<meta type=article>
  <title | Strict mode>
  <author | Enscribe>
</meta>

<config strict-mode=canonical />

<section | Only canonical tags interpret>

This document is in canonical strict mode: both the markdown and the sigil
registers are off, so only canonical named tags interpret. Characters that would
normally be markdown or a sigil pass through as themselves and are flagged with a
visible lint — a nudge toward the canonical form.

A literal *asterisk pair* is not emphasis here, and a sigil <# like this #> is not
a heading; both are flagged. A markdown link [text](https://example.com) and an
image ![alt](image.png) are flagged too — each has a canonical form (an anchor and
a figure). A leading marker on its own line is flagged too:

# This is not a heading in canonical mode

The canonical forms still interpret. This <a https://enscribe.dev | link> works,
and a list is written with the canonical <li> marker, which stays live in
canonical mode (the sigil and markdown item forms would be flagged instead):

<list>
<li> A first item, written canonically.
<li> A second item.
</list>

The sup/sub math shortcuts also stay live — they are not part of the sigil
register — so x^{2} and a_{i} still render as superscript and subscript.

<section | The round-trip guarantee>

Because a canonical document uses only the canonical register, it round-trips
Layer 1 to shorthand and back losslessly: there is no ambiguous markdown heading
for the serializer to guess at. The sigil rung — markdown off, sigils live —
shares this property; only the default off mode is lossy by design.
